`johnsonElongatedTriangularCupola_nu_shared_list`: **every system of `johnsonElongatedTriangularCupola_nu_roots_list` has its one root at the scale
`ν Johnson.ElongatedTriangularCupola.polyhedron`** (`PolynomialSystem.IsRootEq`, `Modules/System/PolyRootEq.lean`): links from root 0 reach each of the roots 0, 1. `johnsonElongatedTriangularCupola_nu_reps_list`:
the same for the system of root 0 alone (one picture class: the roots {0, 1}), and `johnsonElongatedTriangularCupola_nu_reps` the statement in Mathlib's terms alone (`IsPolyRootEq`):
`ν Johnson.ElongatedTriangularCupola.polyhedron` is the first coordinate of the root of each of these systems, each with exactly one root in its
box; it implies `IsPolyRootMax` (`IsPolyRootEq.isPolyRootMax`). The roots' scales are shown equal by links (`Root.linkGroupsOk`, evaluated by compiled code in `Nieuwland.Certs.NieuwlandJohnsonElongatedTriangularCupola.links_ok`): isometries of the solid that take one root's contacts onto another's give a pose map taking the first root's zero to a zero of the other's system inside the other's Krawczyk box, where its zero is unique (`Root.scale_eq_of_linksOk`, `Modules/Root/LinkCheck.lean`; `Root.isRootEq_of_links`, `Modules/Root/Reps.lean`). The representatives are the first root of each class of roots whose pictures (the hole's silhouette and the placed plug's) are congruent: a fact of the data measured in floating point (`lean/scripts/links.py`), not a theorem; the links show the scales equal either way.
Generated by `lean/scripts/exact_n.py` from the theorems of `Nieuwland.Certs.NieuwlandJohnsonElongatedTriangularCupola`, `Nieuwland.Certs.NieuwlandJohnsonElongatedTriangularCupola.Exact` and
`Nieuwland.Certs.NieuwlandJohnsonElongatedTriangularCupola.Roots`, which it does not change.
-/

